  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout suc">RESOLVING THE PREVIOUS POSTER'S FLAG: §334(2a) is now fully confirmed – it covers audit committee members who fail to properly monitor auditor independence under specific EU Audit Regulation (537/2014) provisions, or who base an auditor-appointment recommendation on a selection process that doesn't meet the Regulation's own requirements. This is the underlying administrative offence §333a escalates into a crime under bribery or persistent-repetition conditions.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">The floor beneath the entire criminal-provisions cluster</div>
      <div class="principle">§§331 through 333a require genuine intent (or, narrowly, heightened negligence) to trigger criminal liability. §334 is the administrative-fine floor underneath all of it – capturing the SAME categories of substantive failure (measurement errors, auditor disqualification, audit committee lapses) where the required criminal mental state simply isn't present, ensuring virtually NO violation of this Book's core provisions escapes some form of legal consequence entirely.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §334 MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Proportional enforcement</strong> – genuine negligence gets an administrative fine; genuine intent gets criminal exposure, mirroring the SAME degree-of-fault tiering this series has now traced through §323, §331a, and §332</span>
        <span><strong>Comprehensive coverage</strong> – §334(1)'s enormous cross-reference list touches nearly EVERY substantive provision this entire poster series has documented</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §334 covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>334(1)</span><span>Management/supervisory board negligent violations of core recognition/measurement/presentation rules</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>334(2)</span><span>A disqualified auditor issuing an opinion anyway</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>334(2a)</span><span>Audit committee independence-monitoring/selection-process failures</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>334(3)-(4)</span><span>Tiered fine amounts, a disgorgement-style escalation, and enforcement authority</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – Three Distinct Täterkreise Across One Provision</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Management/supervisory boards, auditors, and audit committee members – all in one provision</div>
      <div class="principle">§334 genuinely spans THREE separate populations this series has covered across multiple posters: §334(1) binds management/supervisory board members (the SAME Täterkreis as §331); §334(2) binds the auditor personally; §334(2a) binds audit committee members specifically (the SAME narrow Täterkreis as §333a). This breadth is itself notable – §334 is structurally the administrative mirror of the entire criminal cluster, rather than a single narrow provision.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§334(1) – Negligence-Level Violations of Recognition, Measurement, and Presentation Rules</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">An enormous cross-reference list, touching nearly everything this series has documented</div>
      <div class="principle">§334(1) makes it an Ordnungswidrigkeit for management or supervisory board members of a Kapitalgesellschaft to violate – in terms of form OR content – an extensive list of provisions this series has covered in detail, including §243(1)-(2) (general measurement principles), §§244-251 (language, currency, signature, completeness, and the entire recognition Titel), §253 (initial/subsequent measurement, extensively – nearly every sentence of it), §254 (hedge accounting), §256a (currency translation), and §264(1a)/(2) (the expanded corporate Jahresabschluss and true-and-fair remedy). This is genuinely one of the longest cross-reference lists in all of HGB, and this poster presents its STRUCTURE and PURPOSE rather than exhaustively reproducing every sub-clause – consistent with how the earlier §285 poster handled a similarly extensive list.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example – Intent (§331) vs Negligence (§334(1)) for the Same Underlying Error</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Identical substantive mistake, radically different legal consequence depending on mental state</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Two companies omit the same mandatory provision, for different reasons</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Recall Example 1 from the earlier §331 poster: a company's management deliberately omits a known, mandatory §249 Rückstellung to flatter results ahead of a refinancing. A SECOND, unrelated company's management genuinely, honestly OVERLOOKS an equally mandatory provision, due to a simple internal review oversight, with no intent to mislead anyone.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The FIRST company's management faces criminal exposure – imprisonment up to three years or a fine – under §331 Nr.1, given the DELIBERATE, knowing omission. The SECOND company's management faces only an ADMINISTRATIVE fine under §334(1) – the SAME underlying category of substantive error (a §249 recognition failure), but WITHOUT the intent §331 requires, the consequence is confined to the administrative sphere rather than the criminal one.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§334(2) – Disqualified Auditors Issuing an Opinion Anyway</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">An administrative counterpart to §319's own exclusion grounds</div>
      <div class="principle">§334(2) makes it an Ordnungswidrigkeit for a person (or the audit firm they work for) to issue a Bestätigungsvermerk (§322(1)) for a Jahresabschluss, an Einzelabschluss under §325(2a), or a Konzernabschluss requiring statutory audit, DESPITE being disqualified under §319(2), (3), or (5), or §319b(1) sentences 1 or 2 – the SAME independence-exclusion grounds established in the earlier Audit poster. This complements §332's own criminal offence (incorrect reporting/opinions) with a SEPARATE administrative offence specifically targeting the PRIOR question of whether the auditor was even eligible to serve at all.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The EU Art.17(3) Rotation Ground – Tying Back to §316a</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A second disqualification ground, specifically for PIE rotation limits</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§334(2) sentence 2 supplies a SECOND, genuinely distinct disqualification ground: an auditor (or their firm) issuing an opinion despite being barred from conducting the audit under Article 17(3) of the EU Audit Regulation (537/2014) – the specific mandatory audit-firm ROTATION rule for public-interest entities that this series first flagged when covering §316a in the earlier Audit poster. An auditor who has simply served TOO LONG at the same PIE client, beyond the EU's own maximum tenure rules, commits this SPECIFIC violation by continuing to issue opinions regardless.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example – an Auditor Exceeding the PIE Rotation Limit</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Tenure alone, not audit quality, is the trigger</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: An audit firm continuing past its maximum PIE engagement period</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An audit firm has served a listed, capital-market-oriented client (a PIE) for a period exceeding the EU Audit Regulation's own maximum permitted engagement duration under Article 17(3), without the specific extension/tendering conditions that would otherwise permit continued service. The firm nonetheless issues a further Bestätigungsvermerk for the current year.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the audit itself being genuinely well-conducted and the resulting opinion being entirely accurate, the firm's continued service PAST the rotation limit itself satisfies §334(2) sentence 2 – the violation is rooted purely in TENURE, entirely independent of the actual quality or accuracy of the audit work performed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§334(2a), Fully Resolved – Audit Committee Independence-Monitoring Failures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The provision §333a escalates – now confirmed in full</div>
      <div class="principle">§334(2a) makes it an Ordnungswidrigkeit for an audit committee member (established under §324(1) sentence 1) to: (1) fail to properly monitor the auditor's or audit firm's independence per the specific standards of EU Audit Regulation Articles 4(3), 5(4), or 6(2); OR (2) submit an auditor-appointment recommendation that fails to meet the Regulation's own Article 16(2) requirements, or that rests on an underlying selection process itself failing to meet Article 16(3)'s own requirements. This is EXACTLY the underlying conduct the previous poster on §333a flagged as needing dedicated verification – now fully confirmed.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – an Audit Committee's Flawed Selection Process</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A procedural failure in HOW the auditor was chosen, not the choice itself</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A recommendation resting on an inadequate selection process</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An audit committee at a PIE recommends the appointment of a specific auditor to the shareholders, but the underlying SELECTION process it relied upon genuinely failed to meet the EU Audit Regulation's own Article 16(3) tendering/comparison requirements – for instance, failing to genuinely solicit and fairly compare competing proposals as the Regulation requires.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Even if the AUDITOR ultimately selected is entirely well-qualified and independent, the audit committee's OWN failure to follow a properly compliant selection process satisfies §334(2a) – this offence targets the PROCESS the committee followed, not merely the ultimate outcome.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§334(3) – the Tiered Fine Structure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Independence and audit-committee failures draw a substantially higher ceiling</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-12"><span>Violation category</span><span>Maximum fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-12"><span>§334(2) S.1 Nr.1, S.2, and §334(2a) – auditor disqualification, rotation violation, audit committee failures</span><span><strong>€500,000</strong></span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-12"><span>§334(1) and §334(2) S.1 Nr.2 – ordinary substantive/measurement violations</span><span><strong>€50,000</strong></span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">A genuinely tenfold difference – reflecting the legislature's judgement that independence-integrity failures (auditor disqualification, rotation breaches, audit committee lapses) warrant a materially higher deterrent ceiling than ordinary substantive measurement errors, even at the merely administrative level.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example – Contrasting the Two Fine Tiers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A tenfold gap between two genuinely different categories of failure</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A measurement error versus an independence breach</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Company A's management negligently misapplies §253's own measurement rules, resulting in a material but honestly-made overstatement. Company B's auditor, meanwhile, negligently fails to recognise their own disqualification under §319 before issuing an opinion.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Company A's management faces an administrative fine of up to <strong>€50,000</strong> under §334(1). Company B's auditor faces a MAXIMUM fine of up to <strong>€500,000</strong> under §334(2) – a TENFOLD higher ceiling for the independence-integrity failure, even though BOTH scenarios involve genuine negligence rather than intent.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>The Capital-Market-Oriented "Higher Of" Disgorgement Escalation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Ensuring the fine can exceed a fixed cap where the violation was genuinely lucrative</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§334(3) sentence 2 provides that WHERE the Kapitalgesellschaft is capital-market-oriented (per §264d), the maximum fine in §334(1) cases is instead the HIGHER of specific alternative amounts – one of which is TWICE the economic advantage gained from the violation itself (calculated as profits earned plus losses avoided, which MAY be estimated where precise figures are unavailable). This is a genuinely important "disgorgement-style" mechanism: a fixed cap could otherwise be trivially absorbed as a cost of doing business by a company for whom the violation generated benefits far exceeding the nominal ceiling – the "twice the benefit" alternative ensures the fine can always exceed whatever was actually gained.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example – a Fine Exceeding the Fixed Cap</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A lucrative violation, met with a proportionately larger fine</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 5: A capital-market-oriented company avoiding significant losses through a measurement violation</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A capital-market-oriented company negligently violates §334(1)'s underlying provisions in a way that allowed it to avoid recognising approximately €120,000 of losses that should genuinely have been reflected in its accounts, materially improving its reported financial position for a period.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Rather than the flat €50,000 ceiling, the fine may instead reach up to TWICE the €120,000 economic advantage – <strong>€240,000</strong> – under §334(3) sentence 2's disgorgement-style escalation, ensuring the fine genuinely exceeds whatever benefit the company gained from the violation, rather than potentially falling short of it.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>§334(4) – Two Different Enforcement Authorities</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A split jurisdiction, matching each provision's own subject matter</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§334(4) confirms that, for §334(1) and §334(2a) cases, the Bundesamt für Justiz – the SAME Federal Office of Justice already established in the earlier Disclosure poster as the authority pursuing Ordnungsgeld proceedings for late or missing filings – has jurisdiction. Auditor-specific §334(2) cases fall instead to APAS (the Abschlussprüferaufsichtsstelle, the auditor oversight body), reflecting a sensible division of labour: general company-side violations to the general enforcement authority, auditor-specific violations to the specialised auditor-oversight body.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>The Full Intent/Negligence Spectrum, Completed</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every degree of fault, for every relevant party, now accounted for</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Party</span><span>Negligence</span><span>Intent</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Company management</span><span>§334(1) – up to €50,000 (or disgorgement-based, for PIEs)</span><span>§331 – up to 3 years' imprisonment</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Bilanzeid signer</span><span>§334(3) – up to €50,000 (ordinary negligence)</span><span>§331a – up to 5 years (or 2 years for Leichtfertigkeit)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Auditor (accuracy)</span><span>–</span><span>§332 – up to 3/5 years</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Auditor (independence)</span><span>§334(2) – up to €500,000</span><span>Generally addressed via professional/regulatory sanctions rather than a dedicated HGB criminal offence</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Audit committee</span><span>§334(2a) – up to €500,000</span><span>§333a – up to 1 year (bribery or persistence only)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This table completes the full accountability architecture this poster series has now documented across §§331–334 – every relevant party, at every degree of fault, faces a proportionate legal consequence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Estimating the "economic advantage" for §334(3) sentence 2's disgorgement calculation where precise figures are genuinely difficult to isolate</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Navigating §334(1)'s enormous cross-reference list to determine precisely which specific sub-clause a given substantive error actually falls under</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Administrative enforcement sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with §§331–333a, IFRS itself has no equivalent – administrative fine enforcement is governed entirely by each jurisdiction's own regulatory apparatus, never by an accounting standard. §334 completes this poster series' comprehensive documentation of the "legal infrastructure beneath any accounting framework" theme: even where a group's Konzernabschluss follows EU-IFRS under §315e, the ENFORCEMENT mechanisms this series has now fully documented – criminal (§§331–333a) and administrative (§334) alike – remain entirely HGB's own.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §334 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>§334(1) substantive violation</span><span><span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss</span></span><span>Negligence-level counterpart to §331</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>§334(2) disqualified opinion</span><span><span class="badge bp">Bestätigungsvermerk</span></span><span>Administrative counterpart to §332</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>§334(2a) committee failure</span><span>–</span><span>The oversight process itself, not a specific document</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §334 connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§331</span><span>The intent-based criminal counterpart to §334(1)'s own negligence-based approach to substantive violations</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§319, §316a</span><span>Supplies the independence and PIE rotation rules §334(2) protects</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§333a</span><span>The provision escalating §334(2a)'s own conduct into a crime, under bribery or persistence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§329</span><span>The Bundesamt für Justiz's parallel role in disclosure-filing enforcement, established in the earlier Disclosure poster</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 20 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Ordnungswidrigkeit</div><div class="glossary-def">An administrative offence, punishable by a fine rather than imprisonment – §334's own category throughout.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Wirtschaftlicher Vorteil</div><div class="glossary-def">Economic advantage – the disgorgement-basis figure (profits gained plus losses avoided) for the §334(3) sentence 2 escalation.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">APAS</div><div class="glossary-def">Abschlussprüferaufsichtsstelle – the specialised auditor oversight body with jurisdiction over §334(2) cases.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Art. 17(3) VO (EU) 537/2014</div><div class="glossary-def">The EU Audit Regulation's mandatory audit-firm rotation limit for PIEs, whose breach constitutes a separate §334(2) ground.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Art. 16(2)-(3) VO (EU) 537/2014</div><div class="glossary-def">The EU Audit Regulation's own auditor-selection process requirements, whose breach underlies §334(2a).</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss</span> Individual annual accounts
      <span class="badge bp">Bestätigungsvermerk</span> Audit opinion
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
